Q: SwiftUI Keyboard toolbar not visible when the TextField is hosted inside a UIKit SplitViewController A: <p>I finally got a solution for this after many hours. It's clearly an issue with SwiftUI not correctly attaching the keyboard accessory bar.</p>
<p>Add to where your host controller is instantiated.</p>
<pre class="lang-swift prettyprint-override"><code>override func viewDidLoad() {
        super.viewDidLoad()
    Task { @MainActor in
            self.keyboardAccessoryFix = LoginKeyboardAccessoryFix(host: hostController)
    }
}
</code></pre>
<p>actual class</p>
<pre><code>private final class LoginKeyboardAccessoryFix {

    private weak var host: UIViewController?
    private var observer: NSObjectProtocol?

    init(host: UIViewController) {
        self.host = host
        observer = NotificationCenter.default.addObserver(
            forName: UIResponder.keyboardDidShowNotification,
            object: nil,
            queue: .main
        ) { [weak self] _ in self?.repair() }
    }

    deinit {
        observer.map(NotificationCenter.default.removeObserver)
    }

    private func repair() {
        observer.map(NotificationCenter.default.removeObserver)
        observer = nil
        guard let host else { return }

        // The override must resolve to a DIFFERENT style than currently shown, else no trait change
        // fires; toggle against the resolved style, then restore and reload on the next run loop.
        let originalStyle = host.overrideUserInterfaceStyle
        host.overrideUserInterfaceStyle = (host.traitCollection.userInterfaceStyle == .dark) ? .light : .dark
        DispatchQueue.main.async {
            host.overrideUserInterfaceStyle = originalStyle
            UIApplication.shared.sendAction(#selector(UIResponder.reloadInputViews), to: nil, from: nil, for: nil)
        }
    }
}
</code></pre>

Q: Silent signed integer overflow before size_t conversion A: <h3>The Root of the Problem</h3>
<p>You seem to be in the habit of using <code>int</code> by default.  Bad idea.  On a lot of targets, 32,768 will overflow an <code>int</code>, and we’re going through another transition like the one to 32-bit CPUs, where on the vast majority of implementations today <code>int</code> is not wide enough to hold an offset into big data.</p>
<p>It’s fundamentally pretty silly to do unchecked signed multiplication and then ask how to get the compiler to warn you that unchecked signed multiplication can overflow.  You already know that!  Either consistently calculate sizes and offsets with unsigned math where overflow isn’t undefined behavior, or consistently use a wide-enough signed value (<code>ptrdiff_t</code>, <code>ssize_t</code>, <code>uintmax_t</code> or <code>unsigned long long</code> will normally work) that overflow is unlikely. I am in camp unsigned, but C++26 will have both checked and saturating signed arithmetic that could make it at least conveivable to allow signed math in secure code.</p>
<h3>Warn About Implicit Conversions</h3>
<p>One thing that would have helped in this specific case is warning about implicit conversions that implicitly change the sign or value.  This wouldn’t catch the potential signed overflow on the right-hand-side if you had declared <code>buggy</code> as <code>auto</code> or a signed type.  However, if your solution to signed-integer overflow is to consistently do wide-enough unsigned math, this will usually warn you when you break the rule.</p>
<ul>
<li>On GCC, the flags are <code>-Wconversion -Wsign-conversion</code>. There are several other variations on this, including <code>-Warith-conversion</code>.</li>
<li>On Clang, <code>-Wconversion</code> should suffice, but it also accepts <code>-Wsign-conversion</code> (and <code>-Wno-sign-conversion</code>).</li>
<li>On MSVC, I normally compile with <code>/Wall /external:anglebrackets /external:W0 /wd 4710 /wd 4711</code>. That is: enable all warnings by default, then disable warnings about external headers in angle brackets, then opt out of the useless warnings that a function is or is not selected for inline expansion.</li>
</ul>
<p>With these settings, all three compilers warn about two sign-changing implicit conversions in the intialization of <code>buggy</code>: you declare <code>bufsize</code> as an <code>int</code> but pass it to a function that takes <code>size_t</code>, and you initialize a <code>size_t</code> variable with an <code>int</code> expression. If you recompile for a 32-bit target, you get a third warning about <code>fixed</code>: you calculate a 64-bit unsigned value on the right side, then assign it to a <code>size_t</code> value that is only 32 bits wide.</p>
<h3>For Quality-of-Life</h3>
<p>One habit I have that might help is to declare constants in my top header like <code>MEGA</code> (<code>1'000'000U</code>), <code>MEGAS</code> (<code>1'000'000</code>), <code>MEBI</code> (<code>0x1'00000U</code>) and <code>MEBIS</code> (<code>0x1'00000</code>). This actually makes it easier to write</p>
<pre><code> size_t still_buggy = std::stoi(std::string(buf, bufsize)) * MEBI;
</code></pre>
<p>On some implementations where <code>size_t</code> and <code>unsigned long</code> have the same width, this would solve the integer overflow by widening the return value of <code>stoi</code> to <code>unsigned long</code>.  On others with 32-bit <code>unsigned long</code> and 64-bit <code>size_T</code> (including 64-bit Windows) you might get unsigned overflow,and end up storing a wrapped-around 32-bit result in a 64-bit variable, which is at least well-defined behavior.</p>
<p>If you often find yourself needing a way to make sure that intermediate values are calculated as <code>size_t</code> that’s portable to systems where <code>SIZE_T</code> could be wider or narrower than <code>long</code>, <code>long long</code> or even <code>int</code>, you might use some shorthand like <code>* SIZE_UNIT</code> where <code>SIZE_UNIT</code> is <code>((size_t)1)</code>, or even hack together the <code>123usize</code> notation from Rust as a C++ user-defined literal.</p>
<h3>My Suggestion</h3>
<p>Write a C++23 function that parses a <code>std::string_view</code> to a <code>size_t</code>:</p>
<pre class="lang-cpp prettyprint-override"><code>#include &lt;charconv&gt;
#include &lt;cstddef&gt;
#include &lt;stdexcept&gt;
#include &lt;string_view&gt;

/* Parses buf for a string representing a size_t and returns it, or throws an
 * exception on error.
 */
constexpr std::size_t to_size_t(const std::string_view buf)
{
    std::size_t result = 0;
    const auto err = std::from_chars(buf.data(), buf.data() + buf.length(), result).ec;

    // More specific checks of the error value can go here.
    if (err != std::errc{}) {
        throw std::invalid_argument(&quot;Invalid input in to_size_t&quot;);
    }
    // If this line is reached, (err != std::errc{}) was false. There is no error.
    return result;
}
</code></pre>
<p>You could template-ify it if you want. This has several advantages over <code>fixed</code>:</p>
<ul>
<li>It checks for error</li>
<li>It accepts just about any stringy object, including a <code>char[]</code>, <code>char*</code> and <code>std::string</code></li>
<li>It does not create a deep copy of <code>buf</code> on the heap like constructing a <code>std::string</code> does</li>
<li>If <code>buf</code> is <code>constexpr</code>, it evaluates to a constant expression</li>
<li>IT returns a <code>size_t</code> result even when that is narrower than an <code>unsigned long long int</code></li>
</ul>
<p>This then lets you write:</p>
<pre><code>const size_t not_buggy = to_size_t(buf) * 1024U * 1024U;
</code></pre>
<p>Or, with the constants above:</p>
<pre><code>const size_t not_buggy = to_size-t(buf) * MEBI;
</code></pre>
<p>Much simpler and much safer!</p>

Q: What is the difference between link wrapping and &#39;use client&#39; in Next.js? A: <p>TLDR: The <code>'use client'</code> directive is a feature of React while the <code>Link</code> component is a custom component of the NextJS framework. They're different unrelated things and that serve different unrelated purposes.</p>
<p>It’s been a minute since I’ve used Next, so if someone chimes in here or documents exist that contradict what I say then trust those other sources more. I’m also not familiar with that button component you’re using from what appears to be the <code>mantine</code> package, so depending on how they implement it, that could also make a difference.</p>
<p>If you're creating a component that needs client side features, such as <code>localstorage</code>, then it needs to be a client component (and needs the <code>'use client'</code> directive). Generally, if something doesn't have to be a client component then it probably shouldn't be (as @chhorm-ratha mentioned).</p>
<p>If you want a link for navigating within your NextJS web app then you use the <code>Link</code> component.</p>
<p><code>'use server'</code> really has little to no bearing on the use of <code>Link</code> and vice versa.</p>
<hr />
<p>The rest of this isn't really addressing your question, but more so addresses a possible misunderstanding of the <code>Link</code> and <code>a</code> (anchor) components in the context of the NextJS framework where they serve complimentary purposes to each other.</p>
<ul>
<li>The <code>Link</code> component is for route navigation <em>within</em> your NextJS web app. The <code>Link</code> component <em>can</em> be used to link to external web pages outside of your NextJS web app, but isn't what it's intended for nor should be used for.</li>
<li>The <code>a</code> (anchor) component is for navigation to web pages <em>outisde</em> your NextJS web app. Conversely, the <code>a</code> (anchor) component <em>can</em> be used to link to web pages within your NextJS web app, but shouldn't be used for that as that's the whole purpose of the <code>Link</code> component.</li>
</ul>
<p>In your first code snippet example</p>
<pre class="lang-jsx prettyprint-override"><code>&lt;Link href=&quot;/voice_monitor&quot; passHref legacyBehavior&gt;
  &lt;Button component=&quot;a&quot;&gt;Go to Voice Monitor&lt;/Button&gt;
&lt;/Link&gt;
</code></pre>
<p>You're wrapping a <code>Button</code> in a <code>Link</code> component and then setting the <code>component</code> prop of the <code>Button</code> component nested within the <code>Link</code> component to an <code>a</code> (anchor) element. Generally, most libraries that implement a <code>component</code> prop of a component makes it so that component actually gets rendered in the browser as the component passed to the <code>component</code> prop. So if you were to do something like:</p>
<pre class="lang-jsx prettyprint-override"><code>&lt;Button component=&quot;a&quot;&gt;Go to Voice Monitor&lt;/Button&gt;
&lt;Button component={Link} href=&quot;/voice_monitor&quot;&gt;Go to Voice Monitor&lt;/Button&gt;
</code></pre>
<p>The actual rendered html for that Button component would be:</p>
<pre class="lang-html prettyprint-override"><code>// note the missing href attribute since it wasn't set it in the button component above
&lt;a&gt;Go to Voice Monitor&lt;/a&gt;
&lt;Link href=&quot;/voice_monitor&quot;&gt;Go to Voice Monitor&lt;/Link&gt;
// technically, I believe Link components actually get rendered as anchor elements so the
// line directly above this isn't actually 100% correct to what gets rendered in the browser
</code></pre>
<p>So you're essentially wrapping an <em>anchor</em> element in a <code>Link</code> component which doesn't really make sense and is probably not what you're intending or wanting to do as their functionality overlaps.</p>
<p>Edit: I want to point out that above, saying the <code>'use client'</code> directive is for a &quot;component that needs client side features...&quot; is true, but doesn't actually explain anything and is a bit of a blanket statement doing a lot of heavy lifting. There's a good chance you've probably already read them, but the <a href="https://react.dev/reference/rsc/use-client" rel="nofollow noreferrer">React docs</a> and <a href="https://nextjs.org/docs/app/api-reference/directives/use-client" rel="nofollow noreferrer">NextJS docs</a> on the <code>'use client'</code> directive are rather good as are the <a href="https://nextjs.org/docs/app/api-reference/components/link" rel="nofollow noreferrer">Link component docs</a>. Definitely worth a read if you haven't checked them out already and despite them being relatively good, even after reading them, chances you'll probably have to re-read them and try implementing them several times until it fully clicks.</p>

Q: I can&#39;t split my HSV image into channels (H,S,V) A: <p><a href="https://docs.opencv.org/4.5.1/d2/de8/group__core__array.html#ga8027f9deee1e42716be8039e5863fbd9" rel="nofollow noreferrer"><code>cv::split(InputArray m, OutputArrayOfArrays mv)</code></a> outputs all channels in the output array, so you need only one call</p>
<pre><code>cv::split(img_hsv, channels);
</code></pre>
<p>instead of the three calls</p>
<pre><code>cv::split(img_hsv, &amp;channels[0]);
cv::split(img_hsv, &amp;channels[1]);
cv::split(img_hsv, &amp;channels[2]);
</code></pre>
